// Body Atlas knowledge base. Keyed "kind:id". Each entry: name, where, what, lenses [[label, text]], links [[title, url]].
// Lens labels: body (anatomy/physiology), tradition (the system's own account), evidence (what studies show), practice (how people work with it), symbol.
// Text is a compact synthesis written for this atlas; treat tradition/metaphysical claims as the tradition's account, not as established fact.
(function(){
const W=s=>'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/'+s;
const K={};
const add=(key,e)=>{K[key]=e;};

// ---------- ROOMS (structure) ----------
add('room:whole',{name:'the whole person',where:'Pin above the crown at (0, +1.09).',what:'A target for anything that belongs to the organism as a whole rather than a part — constitution, temperament, general vitality, systemic conditions.',lenses:[['body','Medicine reads the whole through vital signs, blood work and history; systems biology treats the body as coupled networks (nervous, endocrine, immune) rather than independent organs.'],['tradition','Every system on this plate has a word for the whole: prakriti (Ayurveda), the shen–qi–jing triad (Chinese medicine), the aura (esoteric), the Type and Authority (Human Design).'],['practice','Use this pin for notes that do not localise: sleep, energy, mood, constitution, whole-body protocols.']],links:[['Systems biology',W('Systems_biology')]]});
add('room:skin',{name:'skin & fascia',where:'The silhouette itself — the unit capsule from soles (y −1) to crown (y +1).',what:'Skin is the largest organ (about 1.5–2 m², ~15 % of body mass): barrier, thermostat, immune outpost and the body\'s biggest sense organ. Under it, fascia — the continuous collagen web that wraps every muscle, nerve and organ — is now studied as a body-wide tensional and sensory system.',lenses:[['body','Three layers (epidermis, dermis, hypodermis), millions of mechanoreceptors and free nerve endings, vitamin D synthesis, sweat and sebum. Fascia is richly innervated and contains contractile cells; it transmits force between muscle groups.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the skin is the domain of the Lung and of wei qi, the defensive layer; sweating regulates it. Ayurveda: skin reflects rasa and rakta dhatu and the doshas (dry–vata, red/hot–pitta, oily–kapha). Astrology: Saturn rules skin, bones and boundaries.'],['evidence','Fascia research (Fascia Research Congress, Schleip, Stecco) supports fascia as a sensory organ and force-transmitting network; Anatomy Trains lines are a useful model, partly supported by dissection studies.'],['practice','Touch, dry brushing, cupping, gua sha, myofascial release, cold and heat, and interoceptive work all enter through this layer. It is also the boundary layer for anyone whose sensory gain runs high.']],links:[['Skin',W('Human_skin')],['Fascia',W('Fascia')],['Anatomy Trains',W('Anatomy_Trains')]]});
add('room:crown',{name:'crown · vertex',where:'Top of the skull, (0, +0.985). Meets the crown chakra and the HD Head center.',what:'The vertex of the skull, where the sagittal and coronal sutures meet (bregma, the soft anterior fontanelle in infants). In almost every contemplative tradition it is the door: where the self meets what is larger than the self.',lenses:[['body','Bone (parietal), scalp, the superior sagittal sinus draining venous blood just beneath. Du 20 (Baihui, "hundred meetings") sits here in acupuncture.'],['tradition','Sahasrara, the thousand-petalled lotus; the Taoist Baihui; the Sikh dasam duar (tenth gate); the Tibetan aperture of Brahma used in phowa practice at death. Human Design places the Head center (pressure to know) here.'],['practice','Crown-focused meditation, light visualisation, and the acupuncture point Baihui for lifting qi, clarity and prolapse.']],links:[['Baihui (GV20)',W('Baihui')],['Sahasrara',W('Sahasrara')]]});
add('room:brain',{name:'brain',where:'(0, +0.88), inside the skull; continuous with the spinal cord.',what:'About 1.3–1.4 kg, ~86 billion neurons and as many glia, using roughly a fifth of the body\'s energy at rest. It constructs perception, predicts the body\'s needs, and holds the models we call self.',lenses:[['body','Cerebrum (cortex and deep nuclei), cerebellum, brainstem. The hypothalamus–pituitary axis makes the brain the master endocrine organ; the brainstem houses the autonomic and vagal nuclei that set the body\'s internal weather.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine calls the brain the "sea of marrow", nourished by Kidney essence; shen (spirit, mind) is housed in the Heart, not the brain. Ayurveda: seat of prana and the majja dhatu; the brow and crown chakras map onto pituitary and pineal in many modern syntheses.'],['evidence','Interoception (Craig, Barrett): insula and anterior cingulate integrate body signals into feeling; predictive-processing models treat emotion as the brain\'s best guess about the body. Neuroplasticity is robustly demonstrated; "left-brain/right-brain" personality is not.'],['practice','Sleep, movement, novelty and social contact are the best-evidenced brain care. Neurofeedback, meditation and breathwork measurably shift network activity.']],links:[['Brain',W('Human_brain')],['Interoception',W('Interoception')]]});
add('room:face',{name:'face',where:'(0, +0.79); eyes, nose, mouth, jaw.',what:'The body\'s display and intake surface — expression, speech, breath, food — and in several traditions a diagnostic mirror of the organs.',lenses:[['body','Facial nerve (VII) drives expression, trigeminal (V) carries sensation and moves the jaw. The face is densely mapped in the sensory cortex. Cranial nerves make it the most direct external window onto the brainstem.'],['tradition','Mien shiang (Chinese face reading) maps organs onto zones: forehead–bladder/intestines, between the brows–liver, nose–heart/spleen, cheeks–lungs, chin–kidneys and hormones. Mars rules the face and head in medical astrology (Aries).'],['practice','Facial acupuncture and gua sha; facial expression as a lever on state (the face–vagus link in polyvagal theory); masking as a lived, effortful practice for many neurodivergent people.']],links:[['Facial nerve',W('Facial_nerve')],['Face reading',W('Physiognomy')]]});
add('room:throat',{name:'throat',where:'(0, +0.69): larynx, pharynx, thyroid, upper oesophagus and trachea.',what:'The narrow pass where air, food, voice and the great vessels to the head all share a few centimetres. Voice, swallowing and the thyroid\'s metabolic thermostat live here.',lenses:[['body','Larynx and vocal folds (vagus, via the recurrent laryngeal nerve), thyroid and parathyroids, carotid bodies sensing blood oxygen, the pharyngeal plexus. Swallowing needs six cranial nerves.'],['tradition','Vishuddha, the throat chakra: expression, truth, purification. Human Design: the Throat center, where everything becomes manifest as word or deed. Chinese medicine: Ren 22–23, the Lung and Stomach channels pass here. Venus rules the throat and neck (Taurus).'],['practice','Chanting, humming and toning stimulate the vagus and vocal apparatus; speech and voice therapy; thyroid testing when energy, temperature and mood drift together.']],links:[['Larynx',W('Larynx')],['Thyroid',W('Thyroid')],['Vishuddha',W('Vishuddha')]]});
add('room:heart',{name:'heart',where:'(+0.03, +0.42), slightly left of midline behind the sternum.',what:'A ~300 g muscular pump beating ~100,000 times a day, with its own pacemaker, its own nervous system (the intrinsic cardiac ganglia) and the body\'s strongest rhythmic electromagnetic field. Across cultures it is the organ of feeling and of the self.',lenses:[['body','Four chambers, coronary arteries, the sinoatrial node. Heart-rate variability (HRV) reflects vagal tone and is one of the best non-invasive windows on autonomic state. The heart secretes hormones (ANP, BNP) and sends more signals to the brain than it receives.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the Heart houses shen (spirit-mind) and governs blood and vessels; the Emperor. Anahata, the heart chakra: love, compassion, the meeting of upper and lower. Sufi qalb, the Sacred Heart, the Egyptian weighing of the heart. Human Design\'s Heart/Ego center is willpower, not affection. Sun rules the heart.'],['evidence','HRV biofeedback and slow breathing (~6/min) reliably improve vagal tone and mood. Claims about heart "intuition" or heart-field communication between people are not established.'],['practice','Coherence breathing, loving-kindness meditation, cardiovascular exercise; in dysautonomia, tracking HR on standing is a direct measurement of this room\'s regulation.']],links:[['Heart',W('Heart')],['Heart rate variability',W('Heart_rate_variability')],['Anahata',W('Anahata')]]});
add('room:lungs',{name:'lungs & diaphragm',where:'(0, +0.5), filling the rib cage on both sides of the heart; diaphragm at about y +0.38.',what:'Two spongy organs with ~70 m² of gas-exchange surface, moved by the diaphragm — the only muscle whose rhythm is both automatic and fully voluntary, which is why breath is the universal doorway between traditions.',lenses:[['body','Bronchial tree, alveoli, pulmonary circulation. The diaphragm is driven by the phrenic nerve and its rhythm tunes blood pressure, heart rate (respiratory sinus arrhythmia) and vagal tone. The lungs also filter clots and metabolise hormones.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the Lung governs qi and breath, the skin and wei qi, and the emotion grief; paired with the Large Intestine (Metal). Ayurveda: seat of prana vayu and kapha. Mercury rules the lungs and Gemini the arms and chest.'],['evidence','Slow breathing measurably shifts autonomic balance; breath-based interventions have moderate evidence for anxiety, blood pressure and pain. Nasal breathing increases nitric oxide.'],['practice','Pranayama, box breathing, coherent breathing, Buteyko, Wim Hof; respiratory physiotherapy; singing.']],links:[['Lung',W('Lung')],['Diaphragm',W('Thoracic_diaphragm')],['Pranayama',W('Pranayama')]]});
add('room:solar-plexus',{name:'solar plexus',where:'(0, +0.31), the epigastrium just below the sternum.',what:'The celiac plexus — a dense knot of sympathetic ganglia around the aorta that innervates most of the abdominal organs. Anatomically a nerve centre, experientially the place where fear and excitement are felt.',lenses:[['body','Celiac ganglia, the greater splanchnic nerves and vagal branches; supplies stomach, liver, spleen, pancreas, kidneys, adrenals and gut. A blow here provokes the "winded" spasm.'],['tradition','Manipura, the navel/solar-plexus chakra: fire, will, digestion, personal power. Human Design\'s Solar Plexus center is the emotional wave and, when defined, emotional Authority. Chinese medicine: Ren 12 (Zhongwan) and the Middle Burner.'],['practice','Gut-directed hypnotherapy and breathwork act here; the felt sense in Focusing often lands in this room.']],links:[['Celiac plexus',W('Celiac_plexus')],['Manipura',W('Manipura')]]});
add('room:stomach',{name:'stomach',where:'(+0.07, +0.27), under the left ribs.',what:'A muscular bag that stores, churns and acidifies food (pH ~1.5–3.5), begins protein digestion and meters chyme into the small intestine. Its mucosa and its nerves make it a mood organ as much as a food organ.',lenses:[['body','Parietal cells (acid, intrinsic factor), G cells (gastrin), ghrelin — the hunger hormone — is made here. Vagal fibres govern secretion and motility; stress reliably alters both.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the Stomach "rots and ripens" food and, with the Spleen, is the root of post-natal qi (Earth element; worry and overthinking injure it). Ayurveda: seat of agni, the digestive fire, and pitta. Moon rules the stomach and breasts (Cancer).'],['practice','Meal rhythm, chewing, warm food in Ayurveda and TCM; acupoint ST36 (Zusanli) is the classic tonifying point; the gut–brain axis makes stress work digestive work.']],links:[['Stomach',W('Stomach')],['Gut–brain axis',W('Gut%E2%80%93brain_axis')]]});
add('room:liver',{name:'liver & gallbladder',where:'(−0.1, +0.3), under the right ribs; the gallbladder tucked beneath it.',what:'The largest internal organ (~1.5 kg) and the body\'s chemical plant: it filters blood from the gut, makes bile, stores glycogen, builds proteins and clears hormones and toxins. It regenerates.',lenses:[['body','~500 functions; hepatic portal system; Kupffer cells (immune); bile stored in the gallbladder and released on fat intake. Liver clears cortisol and oestrogen, so hormonal and metabolic complaints often route here.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the Liver stores blood, ensures the smooth flow of qi, governs tendons and eyes, houses the hun (ethereal soul); anger and frustration are its emotion, spring its season (Wood). Ayurveda: pitta organ, seat of rakta dhatu. Jupiter rules the liver (Sagittarius).'],['practice','Alcohol, fructose and drug load are the modern liver stressors; LV3 (Taichong) is the go-to point for stagnation; bitter foods stimulate bile.']],links:[['Liver',W('Liver')],['Liver (TCM)',W('Liver_(Chinese_medicine)')]]});
add('room:spleen',{name:'spleen & pancreas',where:'(+0.15, +0.29), high under the left ribs; pancreas lies behind the stomach.',what:'The spleen filters blood and stages immune responses; the pancreas releases digestive enzymes and, from its islets, insulin and glucagon. Chinese medicine bundles them as one functional organ, "the Spleen".',lenses:[['body','Red pulp recycles old red cells, white pulp holds lymphocytes; splenectomy raises infection risk. Pancreas: exocrine enzymes and endocrine islets — the blood-sugar thermostat.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the Spleen transforms and transports food into qi and blood, holds organs and blood in place, governs muscles and thought (yi); dampness and worry injure it (Earth). Human Design\'s Spleen center is a different thing: the immune-intuitive awareness center, spontaneous and in-the-moment.'],['practice','Warm, cooked, regular meals and moderate exercise are the TCM Spleen prescription; SP6 (Sanyinjiao) is its classic point; blood-sugar stability is the pancreatic one.']],links:[['Spleen',W('Spleen')],['Pancreas',W('Pancreas')]]});
add('room:intestines',{name:'intestines · gut',where:'(0, +0.12), filling the abdomen from the navel down.',what:'About 7 m of small intestine (absorption) and 1.5 m of colon (water, fermentation, microbiome), lined by the largest immune tissue in the body and wired by the enteric nervous system — ~500 million neurons, the "second brain".',lenses:[['body','Villi and microvilli give ~30 m² of absorptive surface. The microbiome (~38 trillion bacteria) makes vitamins, short-chain fatty acids and most of the body\'s serotonin precursors. The gut talks to the brain through the vagus, immune signals and hormones.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: Small Intestine separates pure from impure (paired with Heart); Large Intestine lets go (paired with Lung; grief). The lower dantien and hara sit here — the Japanese seat of self and courage. Svadhisthana overlaps the lower gut. Virgo rules the intestines.'],['evidence','The gut–brain axis is real and bidirectional; microbiome effects on mood in humans are promising but early. Interoceptive sensitivity to the gut varies enormously between people.'],['practice','Fibre diversity, fermented foods, meal timing; gut-directed hypnotherapy has strong evidence for IBS; abdominal breathing and hara work in martial arts and Zen.']],links:[['Enteric nervous system',W('Enteric_nervous_system')],['Gut microbiota',W('Gut_microbiota')]]});
add('room:kidneys',{name:'kidneys & adrenals',where:'(±0.09, +0.2), against the back wall of the abdomen; adrenals capping each kidney.',what:'Two fist-sized filters passing the whole blood volume ~40 times a day, balancing water, salts, acid and blood pressure. Above them the adrenals make cortisol, aldosterone and adrenaline — the stress hormones.',lenses:[['body','~1 million nephrons each; erythropoietin (red cell production) and active vitamin D come from the kidney. Adrenal cortex: cortisol and aldosterone; medulla: adrenaline. The HPA axis ends here.'],['tradition','Chinese medicine: the Kidney stores jing (essence), rules bones, marrow, ears, hair and reproduction, and is the root of yin and yang — fear is its emotion, winter its season (Water). Ayurveda: apana vayu and the pelvic bowl. Venus/Libra rule the kidneys and lower back.'],['practice','Hydration and salt in dysautonomia; KI1 (Yongquan) on the sole and KI3 (Taixi) at the ankle are the great grounding and tonifying points; adrenal "fatigue" is not a medical diagnosis, HPA-axis dysregulation is a research topic.']],links:[['Kidney',W('Kidney')],['Adrenal gland',W('Adrenal_gland')],['Kidney (TCM)',W('Kidney_(Chinese_medicine)')]]});
add('room:spine',{name:'spine · spinal cord',where:'The midline from (0, +0.72) down to the sacrum (0, 0); the cord ends around L1–L2 (y ≈ +0.2).',what:'33 vertebrae protecting the spinal cord — the highway between brain and body, and the axis every subtle system runs along: sushumna, Du mai, the HD channel column, the torus axis.',lenses:[['body','Cervical (7), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacrum, coccyx. Discs, facet joints, the cord and 31 pairs of spinal nerves; the sympathetic chain runs alongside. Dermatomes map skin to segments.'],['tradition','Sushumna nadi runs its length with ida and pingala coiling around it; kundalini is pictured rising from the sacrum. Du mai (Governing vessel) climbs the back. The djed pillar of Osiris, the Tree of Life\'s middle pillar, the world axis. Saturn rules the spine and bones (Leo the heart and back).'],['practice','Spinal mobility, yoga, Feldenkrais, osteopathy and chiropractic; spinal breathing (Taoist microcosmic orbit); posture as autonomic lever.']],links:[['Vertebral column',W('Vertebral_column')],['Spinal cord',W('Spinal_cord')]]});
add('room:pelvis',{name:'pelvis · pelvic floor',where:'(0, +0.03), the bowl at the base of the trunk; the perineum is the origin (0, 0).',what:'The bony basin carrying the trunk onto the legs and holding bladder, bowel and reproductive organs; the pelvic floor is the muscular hammock at its base. Its centre point is the atlas origin.',lenses:[['body','Ilium, ischium, pubis, sacrum. Pelvic floor (levator ani, coccygeus) controls continence, supports organs, and co-contracts with the diaphragm in breathing. Sacral parasympathetic nerves (S2–S4) govern bladder, bowel and sexual function.'],['tradition','Muladhara (root chakra) sits at the perineum: earth, survival, safety. Ren 1 (Huiyin) is here — the meeting of yin. The lower dantien, the hara, the Root center in Human Design (pressure, adrenaline). Scorpio rules pelvis and genitals.'],['practice','Pelvic-floor physiotherapy, mula bandha, Kegels and their release; grounding practices literally anchor at this point.']],links:[['Pelvis',W('Pelvis')],['Pelvic floor',W('Pelvic_floor')],['Muladhara',W('Muladhara')]]});
const arm=(side)=>({name:`${side} arm`,where:`From the shoulder at (${side==='left'?'+':'−'}0.22, +0.64) to the fingertips at x ${side==='left'?'+1':'−1'}, y +0.3.`,what:'Shoulder, upper arm, elbow, forearm, wrist. The arm is the body\'s reach: six of the twelve meridians run its length, and it carries the brachial plexus from the neck to the hand.',lenses:[['body','Humerus, radius, ulna; brachial plexus (C5–T1) → median, ulnar, radial nerves. Shoulder mobility depends on the scapula gliding on the ribcage.'],['tradition','Arm meridians: Lung, Heart and Pericardium (yin, inside), Large Intestine, Small Intestine and Triple Warmer (yang, outside). Gemini rules arms and shoulders. In Anatomy Trains the four arm lines continue the trunk lines into the hand.'],['practice','Shoulder and neck tension commonly reads as arm symptoms; nerve-glide exercises; the arms are the most accessible acupuncture territory.']],links:[['Brachial plexus',W('Brachial_plexus')]]});
add('room:left-arm',arm('left'));add('room:right-arm',arm('right'));
add('room:hands',{name:'hands',where:'Fingertips at x ±1, y +0.3 — the horizontal registration points of the atlas.',what:'27 bones, ~30 muscles, and a quarter of the motor cortex. Six meridians begin or end at the fingertips, and the hand is a reflex map of the whole body.',lenses:[['body','Median, ulnar and radial nerves; the fingertips have the densest touch receptors in the body after the lips. Fine motor control and tool use shaped the human brain.'],['tradition','Meridian endpoints: LU11 thumb, LI1 index, PC9 middle, TW1 ring, HT9 / SI1 little finger. Palmar reflexology mirrors the body onto the palm. Mercury rules the hands. Mudras encode meaning in finger positions; palmistry reads character from the lines. Laogong (PC8) in the palm is the qigong emitting point.'],['practice','Hand reflexology, mudra, acupressure on the jing-well points, grip strength as a marker of general health.']],links:[['Hand',W('Hand')],['Mudra',W('Mudra')]]});
const leg=(side)=>({name:`${side} leg`,where:`From the hip to the sole; mid-patella at (${side==='left'?'+':'−'}0.1, −0.5), sole at y −1.`,what:'Femur, tibia, fibula — the longest bones — and the largest muscles. Six meridians run the leg; the sciatic nerve is the body\'s widest.',lenses:[['body','Lumbosacral plexus → femoral, obturator, sciatic (tibial and peroneal). Standing is an active balancing act driven by calf muscle pumps that return blood to the heart — the mechanism that fails in orthostatic intolerance.'],['tradition','Leg meridians: Spleen, Liver and Kidney (yin, inside), Stomach, Gallbladder and Bladder (yang, outside/back). Sagittarius the thighs, Capricorn the knees, Aquarius the shins and ankles. Legs are the earth pole of the body in most chakra systems.'],['practice','Calf raises, compression and leg crossing counter blood pooling; ST36 below the knee is the most-used point in acupuncture; walking meditation.']],links:[['Sciatic nerve',W('Sciatic_nerve')]]});
add('room:left-leg',leg('left'));add('room:right-leg',leg('right'));
add('room:feet',{name:'feet',where:'Soles at y −1 — the vertical registration line. KI1 (Yongquan) at the centre of each sole.',what:'26 bones and over 100 muscles, tendons and ligaments per foot, ~7,000 nerve endings each. The base of standing, the origin of three meridians and the end of three more, and the most-used reflexology map.',lenses:[['body','Arches act as springs; proprioceptors in the sole feed balance; foot problems propagate up the whole kinetic chain.'],['tradition','KI1 "Bubbling Spring" is where Kidney qi rises from the earth; BL67 at the little toe, SP1 / LR1 at the big toe. Plantar reflexology maps the body head-to-toe from toes to heel. The earth star chakra hangs below the feet. Pisces rules the feet.'],['practice','Barefoot time, foot reflexology, grounding/earthing practices, foot soaks in Chinese medicine.']],links:[['Foot',W('Foot')],['Reflexology',W('Reflexology')]]});
add('room:autonomic',{name:'autonomic nervous system',where:'Pin near the upper spine (−0.055, +0.6); in truth a body-wide system.',what:'The involuntary control system: sympathetic (mobilise), parasympathetic (rest, digest, connect) and enteric branches. It sets heart rate, blood pressure, digestion, pupil size, temperature — the body\'s weather.',lenses:[['body','Sympathetic chain ganglia beside the spine (T1–L2); parasympathetic via cranial nerves (mainly vagus) and S2–S4. Dysautonomia (e.g. POTS) is when this system fails to hold blood pressure and heart rate steady on standing.'],['tradition','Polyvagal theory (Porges) reframes the ANS as a hierarchy of safety states — ventral vagal (social), sympathetic (fight/flight), dorsal vagal (shutdown); influential in trauma therapy though contested in neuroscience. Traditional systems read the same terrain as yin/yang balance or vata disturbance.'],['practice','Breathwork, cold exposure, vagal stimulation, orthostatic training, salt and fluids, compression; nervous-system regulation as the shared vocabulary of somatic therapy.']],links:[['Autonomic nervous system',W('Autonomic_nervous_system')],['Dysautonomia',W('Dysautonomia')],['Polyvagal theory',W('Polyvagal_theory')]]});

// ---------- MERIDIANS ----------
const M=(ab,name,pol,el,hours,pts,text,fn,emo)=>add('meridian:'+ab,{name:`${name} meridian (${ab})`,where:text,what:`${pol==='yin'?'Yin':'Yang'} channel of the ${el} element, ${pts} classical points, most active ${hours} in the organ clock.`,lenses:[['tradition',fn],['symbol',`Emotion: ${emo}. Element ${el}; paired with ${pol==='yin'?'its yang partner':'its yin partner'} across the same element.`],['evidence','Meridians have no confirmed anatomical structure; many points coincide with nerve trunks, fascial planes and vessel bundles. Acupuncture has moderate evidence for chronic pain, nausea and some headache; point-specificity is disputed.'],['practice','Trace the channel with the hand in the direction of flow; acupressure on the listed points; stretch the channel (meridian yoga, Shin Tai).']],links:[['Meridians',W('Meridian_(Chinese_medicine)')],['Organ clock',W('Chinese_organ_clock')]]});
M('LU','lung','yin','Metal','3–5 am',11,'From the chest (LU1, below the collarbone) down the inner arm to the thumb (LU11).','Governs qi and respiration, disperses wei qi to the skin, regulates water passages. Signs: cough, shortness of breath, weak voice, frequent colds, skin dryness.','grief, letting go');
M('LI','large intestine','yang','Metal','5–7 am',20,'From the index finger (LI1) up the outer arm and shoulder, across the neck to the side of the nose (LI20).','Receives from the Small Intestine and eliminates; with the Lung it is about release. LI4 (Hegu) is the master point for the face and pain.','grief held as holding on');
M('ST','stomach','yang','Earth','7–9 am',45,'From below the eye (ST1) down the face, throat, chest and abdomen, along the front of the leg to the second toe (ST45).','Receives and ripens food; the front-of-body channel. ST36 (Zusanli) is the great tonifying point for energy and digestion.','worry, overthinking');
M('SP','spleen','yin','Earth','9–11 am',21,'From the big toe (SP1) up the inner leg, abdomen and chest to the side of the ribs (SP21).','Transforms food to qi and blood, holds blood in the vessels, governs muscles. SP6 (Sanyinjiao) crosses three yin channels.','worry, obsession');
M('HT','heart','yin','Fire','11 am–1 pm',9,'From the armpit (HT1) down the inner arm to the little finger (HT9).','Governs blood and vessels and houses shen. HT7 (Shenmen, "spirit gate") at the wrist calms the mind and helps sleep.','joy — and its excess, agitation');
M('SI','small intestine','yang','Fire','1–3 pm',19,'From the little finger (SI1) up the back of the arm, over the scapula and neck to the front of the ear (SI19).','Separates pure from impure — in food and in judgement. Shoulder and neck pain are its territory.','discernment; clarity vs confusion');
M('BL','bladder','yang','Water','3–5 pm',67,'From the inner eye (BL1) over the head and down the back in two lines, through the buttock and back of the leg to the little toe (BL67).','The longest channel; its back-shu points (BL13–BL28) beside the spine reflect every organ. Drawn dashed on the front plate — it lives on the back.','fear, held in the back');
M('KI','kidney','yin','Water','5–7 pm',27,'From the sole (KI1) up the inner ankle and leg, abdomen and chest to below the collarbone (KI27).','Stores essence, roots yin and yang, governs bones, ears and reproduction. KI1 draws energy down; KI3 tonifies.','fear, will');
M('PC','pericardium','yin','Fire','7–9 pm',9,'From beside the nipple (PC1) down the centre of the inner arm to the middle finger (PC9).','The Heart\'s protector and the channel of emotional intimacy; PC6 (Neiguan) is the nausea and anxiety point.','protection, vulnerability');
M('TW','triple warmer','yang','Fire','9–11 pm',23,'From the ring finger (TW1) up the back of the arm, over the shoulder and neck to the outer eyebrow (TW23).','Regulates the three burners (chest, upper and lower abdomen) — fluid and temperature metabolism; no Western organ equivalent. Drawn dashed: partly posterior.','social warmth; threat vigilance');
M('GB','gallbladder','yang','Wood','11 pm–1 am',44,'From the outer eye (GB1) zig-zagging over the side of the head, down the side of the trunk and leg to the fourth toe (GB44).','Governs decision-making and the sides of the body; GB20 at the skull base and GB34 below the knee are workhorse points.','indecision, resentment');
M('LR','liver','yin','Wood','1–3 am',14,'From the big toe (LR1) up the inner leg, around the genitals and up to the lower ribs (LR14).','Smooth flow of qi, blood storage, tendons and eyes. LR3 (Taichong) moves stagnation — irritability, PMS, tension headaches.','anger, frustration');

// ---------- EXTRAORDINARY VESSELS ----------
add('vessel:Du',{name:'Du mai · Governing vessel',where:'Posterior midline: perineum → sacrum → spine → crown (Du 20) → upper lip.',what:'The "sea of yang channels", running up the back. With Ren mai it forms the microcosmic orbit of Taoist practice.',lenses:[['tradition','Governs all yang; treats spine, brain and back. Du 4 (Mingmen, "gate of life") between the kidneys; Du 14 at C7; Du 20 at the crown; Du 26 under the nose revives consciousness.'],['practice','Microcosmic orbit: breathe qi up the Du on the inhale and down the Ren on the exhale.']],links:[['Governing vessel',W('Governing_Vessel')]]});
add('vessel:Ren',{name:'Ren mai · Conception vessel',where:'Anterior midline: perineum (Ren 1) → navel (Ren 8) → sternum → chin (Ren 24).',what:'The "sea of yin channels", running up the front; governs reproduction, the abdomen and chest.',lenses:[['tradition','Ren 4 and Ren 6 below the navel tonify essence and qi; Ren 12 the stomach; Ren 17 (Shanzhong) the chest and emotions. The dantien lie along it.'],['practice','Abdominal breathing and dantien work happen on this line.']],links:[['Conception vessel',W('Conception_Vessel')]]});

// ---------- FASCIA LINES ----------
const F=(ab,name,text,fn,mer)=>add('fascia:'+ab,{name:`${name} (${ab})`,where:text,what:fn,lenses:[['body','Anatomy Trains (Thomas Myers) traces continuous myofascial connections through dissection — muscle to tendon to bone to the next muscle in line, so tension travels the whole track.'],['tradition',`Rhymes with the ${mer} channel — the same body line found by a different method.`],['evidence','Cadaver studies (Wilke et al., 2016) support continuity for the superficial back line, back functional line and front functional line; weaker for the lateral and spiral lines.'],['practice','Stretch or release along the whole line rather than the sore spot; foam rolling, structural integration, yoga sequences built line by line.']],links:[['Anatomy Trains',W('Anatomy_Trains')],['Fascia',W('Fascia')]]});
F('SFL','superficial front line','Toes → shins → quads → rectus abdominis → sternum → neck flexors → skull behind the ear.','Holds the body upright against gravity from the front; shortens with forward-head posture and protective curling.','Stomach (ST)');
F('SBL','superficial back line','Sole → calf → hamstrings → sacrum → erector spinae → skull → brow.','Keeps the body extended; the line of the forward bend and of a stiff back.','Bladder (BL)');
F('LL','lateral line','Outer foot → outer leg → hip abductors → obliques → intercostals → side of neck.','Braces the body side-to-side and balances left against right.','Gallbladder (GB)');
F('SL','spiral line','Skull → opposite shoulder → across the ribs → opposite hip → outer leg → arch → back up the back.','Wraps the body in a double helix, coordinating rotation and the walking gait.','Gallbladder / Stomach');
F('DFL','deep front line','Inner arch → inner leg → pelvic floor → psoas and diaphragm → pericardium → jaw and tongue.','The body\'s core line; breath, posture and the fight-flight response converge here.','Kidney / Liver / Spleen');
F('SFAL','superficial front arm line','Chest (pectoralis) → inner arm flexors → palm.','Pulling and grasping; shortens with desk posture.','Pericardium / Heart / Lung');
F('DFAL','deep front arm line','Pectoralis minor → biceps → radius → thumb.','Fine control of the thumb and the reach.','Lung (LU)');
F('SBAL','superficial back arm line','Trapezius → deltoid → extensors → back of hand.','Lifting and pushing; the shoulder tension line.','Triple Warmer / Large Intestine');
F('DBAL','deep back arm line','Rhomboids → rotator cuff → triceps → little finger.','Stabilises the shoulder blade and elbow.','Small Intestine / Heart');
add('fascia:*',{name:'fascia line',where:'A continuous myofascial track.',what:'One of Myers\' Anatomy Trains.',lenses:[['body','Continuous myofascial connections traced by dissection.']],links:[['Anatomy Trains',W('Anatomy_Trains')]]});

// ---------- REFLEX ----------
add('reflex:foot',{name:'foot reflexology',where:'Plantar map: toes = head and neck, ball = chest, arch = abdomen, heel = pelvis; inner edge = spine.',what:'The whole body projected onto the soles; pressure on a zone is held to affect the corresponding organ. Systematised by Eunice Ingham (1930s) from Fitzgerald\'s zone therapy; older roots in Egyptian and Chinese foot work.',lenses:[['tradition','Ten longitudinal zones run from toes to head; the left foot mirrors the left side of the body. Both feet together are the body seen from behind.'],['evidence','Reviews find reflexology relaxing and modestly helpful for anxiety and pain, with no evidence that specific zones map to organs. The foot\'s 7,000 nerve endings and the relaxation response are the likely mechanisms.'],['practice','Thumb-walking across zones, 30–60 min sessions; self-treatment with a ball underfoot.']],links:[['Reflexology',W('Reflexology')]]});
add('reflex:hand',{name:'hand reflexology',where:'Palmar map: fingers = head, upper palm = chest, lower palm = abdomen, wrist = pelvis; thumb edge = spine.',what:'The same body map on a more accessible surface — usable anywhere, anytime.',lenses:[['tradition','Korean hand therapy (Koryo Sooji Chim) and Chinese hand acupuncture develop the same idea with their own point systems.'],['practice','Press each zone with the opposite thumb; easy to do through the day.']],links:[['Reflexology',W('Reflexology')]]});
add('reflex:ear',{name:'auricular map',where:'The ear as an inverted fetus: head at the lobe, spine along the antihelix, organs in the concha, legs and feet at the top.',what:'Auriculotherapy — mapped by Paul Nogier (1957) and developed in China. The ear is one of the few body surfaces innervated by the vagus (auricular branch), which is why it is taken seriously in neuromodulation research.',lenses:[['body','The concha carries vagal fibres; the rest of the ear is served by trigeminal, facial and cervical nerves — a small surface with four nerve inputs.'],['evidence','Transcutaneous auricular vagus nerve stimulation (taVNS) is an active research field for epilepsy, depression and inflammation. The NADA five-point protocol for addiction has mixed trial results. Point-organ mapping remains unproven.'],['practice','Ear seeds (vaccaria), press-needles, taVNS devices clipped to the concha.']],links:[['Auriculotherapy',W('Auriculotherapy')],['Vagus nerve stimulation',W('Vagus_nerve_stimulation')]]});

// ---------- MICROSYSTEMS ----------
add('micro:face',{name:'face map · mien shiang',where:'Forehead → bladder and intestines; between brows → liver; nose → heart and spleen; cheeks → lungs; lips → stomach; chin → kidneys and hormones.',what:'Chinese face reading: organs, constitution and character read from the face\'s zones, colours and lines. Adopted by skincare as "face mapping" for acne.',lenses:[['tradition','Colour diagnosis is part of classical Chinese medicine: red = heat, pale = deficiency, yellow = damp, dark = cold or Kidney. Each zone reflects the organ that feeds it.'],['evidence','Some skin changes do reflect internal disease (jaundice, butterfly rash, xanthelasma); the zone-to-organ map itself is untested.']],links:[['Physiognomy',W('Physiognomy')]]});
add('micro:tongue',{name:'tongue diagnosis',where:'Tip → heart and lungs; centre → spleen and stomach; sides → liver and gallbladder; root → kidneys, bladder and intestines.',what:'One of the four pillars of Chinese diagnosis. Body colour, coating, shape and moisture read together; the map assigns regions to organs.',lenses:[['tradition','Pale = deficiency or cold; red = heat; purple = stagnation; thick coat = damp; cracks = dryness or yin deficiency; scalloped edges = Spleen qi deficiency.'],['evidence','Tongue changes accompany dehydration, anaemia, B12 deficiency and infection; AI tongue-image classification is an active research area in China, with inconsistent reproducibility of the organ map.']],links:[['Tongue diagnosis',W('Tongue_diagnosis')]]});
add('micro:iris',{name:'iridology',where:'Each iris as a clock face: the body\'s right side in the right iris, head at 12, feet at 6; concentric rings = stomach, intestines, organs, skin.',what:'Reading health from iris markings, systematised by Ignaz von Péczely (1880s) and Bernard Jensen (1950s). Widely practised in naturopathy.',lenses:[['tradition','Lacunae, radii, pigment spots and the autonomic nerve wreath are read as signs of organ weakness or toxicity in the corresponding zone.'],['evidence','Controlled studies (e.g. Ernst 2000 review) find iridology no better than chance at detecting disease. The iris pattern is stable from infancy — which is why it works for biometric identification, not for tracking health.'],['practice','Included here as a map people use; treat it as a lens, not a test.']],links:[['Iridology',W('Iridology')]]});
add('micro:hara',{name:'hara diagnosis',where:'The abdomen, navel to sternum, divided into organ reflex zones.',what:'Japanese abdominal palpation (fukushin), central to Masunaga\'s Zen Shiatsu: each organ has a diagnostic and a treatment zone on the belly, read by touch for kyo (empty) and jitsu (full).',lenses:[['tradition','The hara is also the seat of self in Japanese culture — hara-gei (belly art), the centre from which martial artists move.'],['practice','Practitioner palpates with the fingers while the client breathes; treatment follows the most kyo–jitsu pair.']],links:[['Shiatsu',W('Shiatsu')]]});

// ---------- TEETH ----------
add('tooth:*',{name:'meridian tooth chart',where:'Each tooth assigned to a meridian pair and its organs.',what:'From biological dentistry (Voll, Kramer, 1950s–70s): incisors → Kidney/Bladder; canines → Liver/Gallbladder; premolars → Lung/Large Intestine (upper) or Stomach/Spleen (lower); molars the reverse; wisdom teeth → Heart/Small Intestine.',lenses:[['tradition','A chronic tooth or root-canal problem is read as a burden on its meridian, and vice versa.'],['evidence','Untested as a diagnostic system; oral infection does affect systemic inflammation and cardiovascular risk in general.'],['practice','Used by holistic dentists to prioritise treatment and to look for non-dental causes of tooth pain.']],links:[['Holistic dentistry',W('Holistic_dentistry')]]});

// ---------- NERVES ----------
add('nerve:vagus',{name:'vagus nerve',where:'Brainstem → neck (beside the carotid) → heart, lungs → stomach, liver, gut down to the transverse colon.',what:'Cranial nerve X, the "wanderer": ~80 % sensory fibres carrying the body\'s state to the brain and the main parasympathetic output to heart and gut. Vagal tone is the physiological backbone of calm.',lenses:[['body','Nucleus of the solitary tract receives it; dorsal motor nucleus and nucleus ambiguus send it. It slows the heart, drives digestion, and signals inflammation to the brain (the inflammatory reflex).'],['tradition','Polyvagal theory splits it into ventral (social engagement) and dorsal (shutdown) branches — a clinically useful map, neuroscientifically contested. In yoga the vagus is increasingly identified with sushumna and the effects of pranayama.'],['evidence','Implanted VNS is approved for epilepsy and depression; taVNS through the ear and slow breathing measurably raise HRV. Vagal-tone claims for cold showers and humming are plausible but small.'],['practice','Extended exhale, humming, gargling, cold on the face, ear stimulation; the "vagus nerve" is the shared language of nervous-system regulation.']],links:[['Vagus nerve',W('Vagus_nerve')],['Vagus nerve stimulation',W('Vagus_nerve_stimulation')]]});
add('nerve:brachial',{name:'brachial plexus',where:'Roots C5–T1 between the neck and armpit; branches run the arm to the hand.',what:'The nerve network of the arm: median, ulnar, radial, musculocutaneous and axillary nerves. Neck and shoulder posture compresses it — thoracic outlet syndrome.',lenses:[['body','Motor and sensory to the whole upper limb; injuries at birth (Erb\'s palsy) or from traction.'],['tradition','The six arm meridians pass through this territory; the scalene and pectoral zones are dense with points.']],links:[['Brachial plexus',W('Brachial_plexus')]]});
add('nerve:sciatic',{name:'sciatic nerve',where:'Roots L4–S3; exits below the piriformis, runs the back of the thigh, splits at the knee into tibial and common peroneal.',what:'The body\'s widest nerve (~2 cm). Sciatica is its irritation — a disc, the piriformis, or the sacroiliac joint.',lenses:[['body','Motor to the hamstrings and everything below the knee; sensory to the back and side of the leg and the foot.'],['tradition','Follows the Bladder meridian almost exactly; BL40 (Weizhong) behind the knee and GB30 (Huantiao) at the hip are its acupuncture treatment points.']],links:[['Sciatic nerve',W('Sciatic_nerve')]]});

// ---------- CHAKRAS ----------
const CH=(id,name,eng,where,el,seed,gland,text,shadow,links)=>add('center:'+id,{name:`${name} · ${eng}`,where,what:text,lenses:[['tradition',`Element ${el}; seed syllable ${seed}. In the Shat-chakra-nirupana (1577) and its Tantric sources, the chakras are meditation supports along sushumna — visualised, not assumed to be anatomical.`],['body',`Modern syntheses pair it with the ${gland}. The mapping is 20th-century (Leadbeater, Woodroffe), not classical.`],['symbol',shadow],['practice','Breath, seed-sound chanting, colour visualisation, asana focused on the region; in trauma-informed work, chakras function as a somatic vocabulary for where feeling lives.']],links:links.concat([['Chakra',W('Chakra')]])});
CH('muladhara','muladhara','root','Perineum, (0, 0) — the atlas origin. 4 petals, red.','earth','LAM','adrenals and pelvic floor','Survival, safety, belonging, the body\'s right to exist. Kundalini is pictured coiled here.','Balanced: grounded, present. Excess: rigidity, hoarding. Deficient: fear, dissociation from the body.',[['Muladhara',W('Muladhara')]]);
CH('svadhisthana','svadhisthana','sacral','Lower abdomen, (0, +0.08). 6 petals, orange.','water','VAM','gonads and the pelvic organs','Pleasure, emotion, sexuality, creativity, flow. "One\'s own abode."','Balanced: feeling and flow. Excess: overwhelm, addiction. Deficient: numbness, rigidity.',[['Svadhisthana',W('Svadhisthana')]]);
CH('manipura','manipura','solar plexus','Epigastrium, (0, +0.25). 10 petals, yellow.','fire','RAM','pancreas and adrenals','Will, agency, digestion of experience, personal power. "City of jewels."','Balanced: confidence, warmth. Excess: domination, burnout. Deficient: shame, passivity.',[['Manipura',W('Manipura')]]);
CH('anahata','anahata','heart','Centre of the chest, (0, +0.42). 12 petals, green (or smoke-coloured in the classical text).','air','YAM','thymus and heart','Love, compassion, grief, the bridge between the three lower and three upper centres. "Unstruck" — the sound not made by two things striking.','Balanced: warmth and boundaries together. Excess: codependence. Deficient: isolation, guardedness.',[['Anahata',W('Anahata')]]);
CH('vishuddha','vishuddha','throat','Throat, (0, +0.69). 16 petals, blue.','space / ether','HAM','thyroid','Expression, truth, listening, purification. "Especially pure."','Balanced: clear voice, honest speech. Excess: talking over. Deficient: swallowing words.',[['Vishuddha',W('Vishuddha')]]);
CH('ajna','ajna','brow · third eye','Between the brows, (0, +0.9). 2 petals, indigo.','light / mind','OM','pituitary (or pineal)','Insight, imagination, intuition, seeing pattern. "Command."','Balanced: clarity. Excess: fantasy, headaches. Deficient: denial, poor memory.',[['Ajna',W('Ajna')]]);
CH('sahasrara','sahasrara','crown','Crown of the head or just above, (0, +1.02). 1,000 petals, violet or white.','beyond the elements','silence (AUM)','pineal','Unity, transcendence, the dissolution of the separate self. Strictly, above the chakra system rather than the seventh of it.','Balanced: meaning, connection. Excess: spiritual bypass. Deficient: cynicism, disconnection.',[['Sahasrara',W('Sahasrara')]]);
add('center:soul star',{name:'soul star · 8th chakra',where:'About a hand above the crown, (0, +1.28); part of the transpersonal set (8–12).',what:'A New Age extension of the seven: the seat of the soul\'s records, karmic patterns and the connection to the higher self. Not in classical Tantric sources.',lenses:[['tradition','Often placed with the earth star as a pair bracketing the body — soul above, earth below — and used as the anchor points of the torus and Merkaba.'],['practice','Visualised as a white or gold star in meditation; opened after the seven are balanced.']],links:[['Chakra (New Age)',W('Chakra#New_Age_and_Western_esotericism')]]});
add('center:earth star',{name:'earth star chakra',where:'Below the feet, (0, −1.28); drops into the earth disc at y −1.4.',what:'The grounding chakra of the transpersonal set: the body\'s anchor into the planet, ancestral lineage and the collective. A New Age addition, widespread in energy healing since the 1990s.',lenses:[['tradition','Paired with the soul star; described as brown, black or magma-coloured; connects to the ley grid and Gaia.'],['practice','Grounding cords, barefoot practice, visualising roots.']],links:[['Earthing',W('Earthing_(alternative_medicine)')]]});
add('center:dantien',{name:'lower dantien',where:'Three finger-widths below the navel and inside the body, (0, +0.05). Drawn dashed so it is not read as svadhisthana.',what:'The "elixir field" of Taoist internal alchemy and qigong — the reservoir where qi is gathered and refined; in Japanese arts the hara or seika tanden, the physical and psychological centre of gravity.',lenses:[['tradition','Three dantien: lower (jing/essence, below the navel), middle (qi, the heart), upper (shen, the brow). Qigong sinks the breath and attention here; internal alchemy circulates from here.'],['body','Coincides with the body\'s centre of mass in standing and with the pelvic floor–diaphragm pressure system — which is why "moving from the hara" works.'],['practice','Abdominal breathing, standing meditation (zhan zhuang), tai chi, aikido.']],links:[['Dantian',W('Dantian')],['Hara',W('Hara_(tanden)')]]});

// ---------- HUMAN DESIGN ----------
const HD=(id,name,shape,where,text,defined,open,body)=>add('hd:'+id,{name:`${name} center`,where,what:text,lenses:[['tradition',`Defined (coloured in a chart): ${defined} Open / undefined: ${open}`],['body',body],['evidence','Human Design (Ra Uru Hu, 1987) synthesises the I Ching, astrology, Kabbalah and the chakras with a neutrino-based transmission story; it has no scientific standing and is best read as a symbolic map, like a tarot spread of the body.'],['practice','Charts are cast from birth time; the map is used for decision-making style (Authority), energy type and where one is conditioned by others.']],links:[['Human Design',W('Human_Design')]]});
HD('head','Head','triangle','Above the brow, (0, +0.955).','Pressure center: the pressure to think, question and be inspired. Gates 64, 61, 63.','consistent mental pressure and inspiration.','takes on others\' questions; learns to enjoy inspiration without needing to resolve it.','Corresponds loosely to the crown; HD treats it as pressure, not awareness.');
HD('ajna','Ajna','triangle','Brow, (0, +0.84).','Awareness center of the mind: conceptualising, opinions, certainty. Gates 47, 24, 4, 17, 43, 11.','a fixed way of processing; mental certainty.','flexible, open-minded, can pretend to be certain.','Named after the brow chakra but framed as cognition rather than intuition.');
HD('throat','Throat','square','Throat, (0, +0.66).','The manifestation center: everything is expressed through it as speech or action. 11 gates — the most connected center.','a consistent voice and way of acting.','pressure to attract attention by speaking; learns to wait for invitation.','The larynx; the point where inner process becomes outer act.');
HD('g','G · Identity','diamond','Chest, (0, +0.5) — between throat and heart.','Identity, direction, love and the "magnetic monopole" that holds the self together. Gates 1, 13, 25, 46, 2, 15, 10, 7.','fixed sense of self and direction.','fluid identity; finds self through people and place; sensitive to environment.','Sits at the heart\'s height; HD separates identity (G) from will (Heart/Ego).');
HD('ego','Heart / Ego','triangle','Right of the sternum, (+0.1, +0.4).','Willpower, ego, material world, promises. A motor. Gates 21, 40, 26, 51.','reliable willpower; can make and keep promises.','nothing to prove; inconsistent will; learns not to over-promise.','Associated with heart, stomach, gallbladder and thymus in HD teaching.');
HD('spleen','Spleen','triangle','Left side, (−0.17, +0.2).','Awareness of the body in the now: intuition, immune system, fear, survival instinct. Gates 48, 57, 44, 50, 32, 28, 18.','consistent intuitive and immune awareness; spontaneous.','holds on to what is not good for it; sensitive to others\' health and fears.','HD ties it to spleen, lymph, immune system — the body\'s alarm.');
HD('sp','Solar Plexus','triangle','Right side, (+0.17, +0.2).','Emotional awareness in waves — hope to pain and back; the emotional Authority when defined. Gates 6, 37, 22, 36, 30, 55, 49.','an emotional wave; clarity comes over time, never in the moment.','amplifies others\' emotions; avoids confrontation.','Kidneys, pancreas, nervous system in HD; the celiac plexus anatomically.');
HD('sacral','Sacral','square','Lower abdomen, (0, +0.16).','Life-force and sexual energy, work capacity, the guttural yes/no response. Generators are defined here. Gates 5, 14, 29, 59, 9, 3, 42, 27, 34.','sustainable energy for work; responds with sounds.','no consistent energy; must rest more; learns when enough is enough.','Reproductive organs; overlaps svadhisthana.');
HD('root','Root','square','Base of the trunk, (0, −0.06).','Pressure center: adrenaline, stress, the drive to act and finish. Gates 53, 60, 52, 19, 39, 41, 58, 38, 54.','a fixed way of handling pressure.','hurries to be free of pressure; learns that pressure is not urgency.','Adrenals; overlaps muladhara.');

// ---------- GATES · HEXAGRAMS · GENE KEYS ----------
const HEX_MEANING=['The Creative — heaven; originating force, strength that begins','The Receptive — earth; devotion, yielding, holding','Difficulty at the Beginning — a sprout pushing through','Youthful Folly — inexperience that must learn','Waiting — nourishment while conditions ripen','Conflict — opposing claims; seek the middle','The Army — organised strength under discipline','Holding Together — union around a centre','Small Taming — gentle restraint','Treading — conduct; walking carefully on the tiger\'s tail','Peace — heaven and earth in harmony','Standstill — stagnation; withdraw','Fellowship — community with others','Great Possession — abundance held rightly','Modesty — the mountain within the earth','Enthusiasm — thunder rising; inspiration that moves','Following — adapting to what leads','Work on What Has Been Spoiled — repair the decayed','Approach — the coming near of the great','Contemplation — the view from the tower','Biting Through — decisive justice','Grace — form and adornment','Splitting Apart — collapse; wait','Return — the turning point; light comes back','Innocence — the unexpected; act without calculation','Great Taming — power held in restraint','Nourishment — the corners of the mouth; care in feeding','Great Exceeding — the ridgepole sags; extraordinary times','The Abysmal — water; danger met with sincerity','The Clinging — fire; clarity that depends','Influence — wooing; attraction','Duration — what endures through change','Retreat — timely withdrawal','Great Power — strength that must be right','Progress — the sun rising over earth','Darkening of the Light — keep your light inside','The Family — the clan; roles and warmth','Opposition — estrangement that can be bridged','Obstruction — the mountain path is blocked; turn inward','Deliverance — release after tension','Decrease — simplicity by giving up','Increase — gain by giving','Breakthrough — resoluteness; the truth breaks out','Coming to Meet — the encounter; watch the first sign','Gathering Together — the assembly around a leader','Pushing Upward — steady growth like a tree','Oppression — exhaustion; hold to your word','The Well — the inexhaustible source','Revolution — moulting; change at the right moment','The Cauldron — transformation and nourishment','The Arousing — thunder; shock that awakens','Keeping Still — the mountain; meditation','Development — gradual progress, like a tree on a mountain','The Marrying Maiden — subordinate position; know your place','Abundance — fullness at noon; it will pass','The Wanderer — the stranger; travel lightly','The Gentle — wind; penetrating influence','The Joyous — the lake; joy shared','Dispersion — dissolution; wind over water','Limitation — measure and rhythm','Inner Truth — sincerity that moves others','Small Exceeding — attend to details','After Completion — order that must be maintained','Before Completion — the crossing not yet made'];
const HD_GATE=['Self-Expression','Direction of the Self','Ordering','Formulization','Fixed Rhythms','Friction','The Role of the Self','Contribution','Focus','Behaviour of the Self','Ideas','Caution','The Listener','Power Skills','Extremes','Skills','Opinions','Correction','Wanting','The Now','The Hunter','Openness','Assimilation','Rationalisation','Spirit of the Self','The Egoist','Caring','The Game Player','Saying Yes','Recognition of Feelings','Leading','Continuity','Privacy','Power','Change','Crisis','Friendship','The Fighter','The Provocateur','Aloneness','Contraction','Growth','Insight','Alertness','The Gatherer','Determination of the Self','Realisation','Depth','Principles','Values','Shock','Stillness','Beginnings','Ambition','Spirit','Stimulation','Intuitive Insight','Vitality','Sexuality','Acceptance','Mystery','Details','Doubt','Confusion'];
const GK=['Entropy → Freshness → Beauty','Dislocation → Orientation → Unity','Chaos → Innovation → Innocence','Intolerance → Understanding → Forgiveness','Impatience → Patience → Timelessness','Conflict → Diplomacy → Peace','Division → Guidance → Virtue','Mediocrity → Style → Exquisiteness','Inertia → Determination → Invincibility','Self-Obsession → Naturalness → Being','Obscurity → Idealism → Light','Vanity → Discrimination → Purity','Discord → Discernment → Empathy','Compromise → Competence → Bounteousness','Dullness → Magnetism → Florescence','Indifference → Versatility → Mastery','Opinion → Far-Sightedness → Omniscience','Judgement → Integrity → Perfection','Co-dependence → Sensitivity → Sacrifice','Superficiality → Self-Assurance → Presence','Control → Authority → Valour','Dishonour → Graciousness → Grace','Complexity → Simplicity → Quintessence','Addiction → Invention → Silence','Constriction → Acceptance → Universal Love','Pride → Artfulness → Invisibility','Selfishness → Altruism → Selflessness','Purposelessness → Totality → Immortality','Half-Heartedness → Commitment → Devotion','Desire → Lightness → Rapture','Arrogance → Leadership → Humility','Failure → Preservation → Veneration','Forgetting → Mindfulness → Revelation','Force → Strength → Majesty','Hunger → Adventure → Boundlessness','Turbulence → Humanity → Compassion','Weakness → Equality → Tenderness','Struggle → Perseverance → Honour','Provocation → Dynamism → Liberation','Exhaustion → Resolve → Divine Will','Fantasy → Anticipation → Emanation','Expectation → Detachment → Celebration','Deafness → Insight → Epiphany','Interference → Teamwork → Synarchy','Dominance → Synergy → Communion','Seriousness → Delight → Ecstasy','Oppression → Transmutation → Transfiguration','Inadequacy → Resourcefulness → Wisdom','Reaction → Revolution → Rebirth','Corruption → Equilibrium → Harmony','Agitation → Initiative → Awakening','Stress → Restraint → Stillness','Immaturity → Expansion → Superabundance','Greed → Aspiration → Ascension','Victimisation → Freedom → Freedom','Distraction → Enrichment → Intoxication','Unease → Intuition → Clarity','Dissatisfaction → Vitality → Bliss','Dishonesty → Intimacy → Transparency','Limitation → Realism → Justice','Psychosis → Inspiration → Sanctity','Intellect → Precision → Impeccability','Doubt → Inquiry → Truth','Confusion → Imagination → Illumination'];
K.gate=g=>({name:`gate ${g}`,where:'One 5.625° slice of the 64-gate wheel; the same slice is I Ching hexagram, Human Design gate and Gene Key.',what:`I Ching: ${HEX_MEANING[g-1]}.`,lenses:[['tradition',`Human Design gate keynote: ${HD_GATE[g-1]}. Activated by a planet\'s position at birth or 88° before; a gate is half a channel — two gates make a defined channel and colour in two centers.`],['symbol',`Gene Key ${g} (Richard Rudd): shadow → gift → siddhi = ${GK[g-1]}. Contemplation moves the frequency from shadow toward siddhi.`],['evidence','The I Ching (c. 1000 BCE, Zhou) is a divination and wisdom text; its 64 hexagrams map to the 64 codons only by count. Human Design and Gene Keys are contemporary syntheses (1987, 2009). Keynotes above are from memory of the published systems — verify against your sources.']],links:[['I Ching',W('I_Ching')],[`Hexagram ${g}`,W('List_of_hexagrams_of_the_I_Ching')],['Gene Keys',W('Gene_Keys')]]});

// ---------- PLANETS ----------
const PL=(n,where,what,rules,body)=>add('planet:'+n,{name:n,where,what,lenses:[['tradition',rules],['body',body],['evidence','Medical astrology is a historical framework (Hippocratic humours → Galen → medieval zodiac man); it shaped Western medicine until the 17th century and has no predictive validity in modern trials.']],links:[['Medical astrology',W('Medical_astrology')],[n,W(n+(n==='Mercury'||n==='Venus'||n==='Mars'||n==='Jupiter'||n==='Saturn'?'_(planet)':''))]]});
PL('Sun','Position on the zodiac ring; example position, not a chart.','The self, vitality, the heart; rules Leo.','Rules the heart, spine and general vitality; hot and dry. The Sun sign is the core identity in modern astrology; in Human Design the Sun carries 70 % of the imprint.','Circadian rhythm is literally solar: light sets cortisol, melatonin and body temperature.');
PL('Moon','Example position on the ring.','Emotion, habit, the body\'s tides; rules Cancer.','Rules the stomach, breasts, womb and fluids; cold and moist. Moon sign = emotional nature; the lunar cycle was the ancient clock of menstruation and madness ("lunacy").','The 29.5-day lunar month approximates the menstrual cycle; studies find weak or no lunar effects on sleep, births or mood.');
PL('Mercury','Example position on the ring.','Mind, speech, nerves, exchange; rules Gemini and Virgo.','Rules the lungs, hands, nervous system and intestines; the messenger between body systems.','Nerve conduction, breath and the gut–brain axis are its modern echoes.');
PL('Venus','Example position on the ring.','Love, pleasure, harmony; rules Taurus and Libra.','Rules the throat, kidneys, veins and skin\'s beauty; warm and moist. Venus placement describes how one relates and what one enjoys.','Oxytocin, the parasympathetic "rest and digest" state.');
PL('Mars','Example position on the ring.','Drive, aggression, heat; rules Aries and Scorpio.','Rules the head, face, blood, muscles and genitals; hot and dry; inflammation, fever, cuts and surgery.','Adrenaline, testosterone, the sympathetic surge.');
PL('Jupiter','Example position on the ring.','Expansion, growth, faith; rules Sagittarius and Pisces.','Rules the liver, thighs, fat and growth; warm and moist; the "greater benefic".','Metabolic expansion — the liver\'s synthesis and storage.');
PL('Saturn','Example position on the ring.','Structure, limit, time; rules Capricorn and Aquarius.','Rules bones, teeth, skin, joints, knees and the spine; cold and dry; chronic and slow conditions. The Saturn return (~29.5 years) is a life-stage marker.','Collagen, bone density, the aging clock.');

// ---------- FIELDS ----------
add('field:aura',{name:'aura · biofield',where:'Three dashed eggs around the body at r 1.3, 1.6 and 2.0 (etheric, emotional, mental) — sizes are conventional, not measured.',what:'The luminous envelope described by clairvoyants (Leadbeater, Brennan) and by traditions as wei qi (Chinese), pranamaya kosha (yoga), or the astral body. Modern energy medicine calls it the biofield.',lenses:[['tradition','Brennan\'s Hands of Light describes seven layers alternating structured and fluid, each linked to a chakra; Theosophy gives etheric, astral, mental, causal. Chinese medicine: wei qi circulates at the surface and just beyond, warming and defending.'],['body','The body does have measurable fields: the heart\'s magnetic field (~1 pT at the chest, detectable by SQUID magnetometers to ~1 m), infrared radiation, and a bioelectric skin potential. None matches the described layers.'],['evidence','Kirlian photography images skin moisture and corona discharge, not an aura. Emily Rosa\'s 1998 test of therapeutic-touch practitioners found no ability to detect a field. Synesthetic "aura seeing" is a documented perceptual phenomenon.'],['practice','Energy healing (Reiki, therapeutic touch, pranic healing) works in this layer; the aura is also a useful metaphor for personal space and sensory boundary.']],links:[['Aura',W('Aura_(paranormal)')],['Biofield',W('Energy_medicine')],['Magnetocardiography',W('Magnetocardiography')]]});
add('field:torus',{name:'torus field',where:'Axis through the spine, crown to perineum; flows out the crown, arcs down around the body and re-enters at the perineum (or the reverse).',what:'The doughnut-shaped flow that a spinning dipole makes — the shape of the earth\'s magnetosphere, a magnet\'s field lines, a smoke ring. Energy teachings picture the human field the same way, with the heart as its centre.',lenses:[['tradition','HeartMath and Drunvalo Melchizedek popularised the human torus; in these accounts it is the heart\'s electromagnetic field extended and organised. The chakras become vortices on its axis; the aura its outer boundary. Many traditions describe the same shape without the word: the microcosmic orbit, the fountain breath.'],['body','The heart\'s field is a dipole, so it really does have a toroidal shape close to the chest — at picotesla strength, far below the earth\'s field. The "fountain" motion also describes cerebrospinal fluid and lymph return in a loose sense.'],['evidence','Field strength, not shape, is the issue: the heart\'s field is measurable but tiny, and there is no evidence of the large, coherent torus described in energy teachings.'],['practice','Fountain / torus breathing: inhale up the spine, exhale out the crown and down around the body back to the feet; used in qigong, HeartMath coherence and many meditation systems.']],links:[['Torus',W('Torus')],['Magnetocardiography',W('Magnetocardiography')],['Magnetosphere',W('Magnetosphere')]]});
add('field:merkaba',{name:'merkaba · star tetrahedron',where:'Two interlocking tetrahedra around the body; drawn as a hexagram at r 2.5. The body-scale star has its apexes a hand above the crown and below the feet.',what:'From Hebrew merkavah, "chariot" — Ezekiel\'s vision and the earliest Jewish mystical tradition (Hekhalot literature, 1st–7th c.). Drunvalo Melchizedek\'s Flower of Life (1990s) reframed it as a counter-rotating light-body vehicle: Mer (light), Ka (spirit), Ba (body).',lenses:[['tradition','Melchizedek: the up-tetrahedron is masculine/electric, the down feminine/magnetic; the 17-breath meditation spins them in opposite directions, generating a 55-foot disc at the base of the spine. Jewish merkavah mysticism is about ascent through the heavenly palaces, not a personal geometry.'],['symbol','The star tetrahedron is the 3D form of the hexagram / Star of David / Shatkona: fire and water triangles, Shiva and Shakti, above and below united.'],['evidence','A geometric meditation image; no physical field is implied or measured.'],['practice','The Mer-Ka-Ba meditation (17 breaths, mudras, visualisation) — a structured breath and imagery practice.']],links:[['Merkabah mysticism',W('Merkabah_mysticism')],['Stellated octahedron',W('Stellated_octahedron')],['Drunvalo Melchizedek',W('Drunvalo_Melchizedek')]]});
add('field:polarity',{name:'polarity · left / right',where:'The midline. +x is the subject\'s left, −x the right; the upper/lower split at mid-body.',what:'Almost every system divides the body into a receptive and an active half, and the sides mostly agree: left–lunar–feminine–yin, right–solar–masculine–yang.',lenses:[['tradition','Yoga: ida (left, lunar, cooling) and pingala (right, solar, heating) coil around sushumna; nadi shodhana balances them. Chinese medicine: left is yang and right yin in some diagnostic conventions (pulse positions differ left and right). Kabbalah: the right pillar is Mercy, the left Severity — as the Tree faces you, so mirrored on the body. Alchemy: Sol and Luna, the rebis. Merkaba: upper tetrahedron masculine, lower feminine.'],['body','Brain lateralisation is real but modest — language mostly left, spatial attention mostly right; the "logical left / creative right" personality split is a myth. The body itself is asymmetric: heart left, liver right, and the two vagal branches serve different organs.'],['evidence','Alternate-nostril breathing shows small measurable effects on autonomic balance; the nasal cycle (nostrils alternating dominance every 1–4 h) is a real physiological rhythm that the yogic account noticed early.'],['practice','Nadi shodhana, cross-lateral movement (Brain Gym, crawling patterns), attending to which side symptoms favour.']],links:[['Nadi',W('Nadi_(yoga)')],['Lateralization of brain function',W('Lateralization_of_brain_function')],['Nasal cycle',W('Nasal_cycle')]]});

window.ATLAS_KNOWLEDGE=K;
})();
